# byte.bot -- the "byte" namespace: the Byte/Nibble/LowNibble/HighNibble
# scalar types (declared below) and byte/bit-domain operations over them.
#
# Every function here is ordinary Botlish with a verified refined result
# primitives core/scalarbits.tcl registers as root natives (bit_and, bit_or,
# bit_xor, shift_left, shift_right -- already in native/lower.tcl's own op
# table, so each compiles inline wherever it is called, module function or
# not). This is deliberately NOT a thin wrapper around a separate native
# with a declared HighNibble/LowNibble/Nibble result type: that shape was
# tried first and does not natively compile from a call site *inside* a
# cross-file module function (which is exactly this file's own shape, and
# web.bot's high_nibble's) -- native lowering's -native-body/-module-fn
# bridge (native/native.tcl) is a syntactic pre-pass over only the entry
# program's own raw core IR, so it can substitute a native called directly
# from an .ir program's top level, but never one called from inside a .bot
# module's own compiled body (core/scalarbits.tcl's header has the full
# trace). Plain functions over already-lowerable primitives sidestep that
# wall entirely. Optional result contracts now preserve each nominal type
# across ordinary module calls; hir/range.tcl derives interval and exact-set
# facts from that semantic type without reanalyzing this body at call sites.
#
#   byte::high_nibble(b)   position-preserving high-half extraction: b&0xF0
#   byte::low_nibble(b)    position-preserving low-half extraction: b&0x0F
#   byte::nibble(x)        positioned nibble (either half) -> logical Nibble
#                          (0..15): ((x&0x0F)|(x>>4))&0x0F -- correct for
#                          any x in Byte's own domain (0..255): recovers x
#                          unchanged when x is already low-positioned
#                          (x<=15, so x>>4=0) and x>>4 when x is high-
#                          positioned (x's low 4 bits are 0 by construction,
#                          so x&0x0F=0) -- one branch-free formula, no
#                          special-casing of which half x came from.
#   byte::complement(b)    one's complement over Byte's 8-bit domain: b^0xFF
#                          (never Int's own arbitrary-precision complement).
#   byte::position_low(n)  Nibble -> LowNibble: representation-preserving.
#   byte::position_high(n) Nibble -> HighNibble: n<<4, positioned.
#   byte::from_int(value: int)  Int -> Byte, errors BelowRange, AboveRange:
#                          the ordinary explicitly-fallible Int -> Byte
#                          conversion (EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md;
#                          replaces the old callable-type-syntax Byte(x),
#                          removed from source -- see that report for why).
#                          A statically provable in-range/out-of-range
#                          argument needs no handler, or is a compile-time
#                          KNOWN-ERROR, respectively -- see
#                          STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md.
#   byte::set(chars)       List[UnicodeChar] -> ImmutableSet[Byte], errors
#                          AboveRange: each char's own Unicode scalar value
#                          (char::codepoint), converted via from_int above
#                          -- never UTF-8 encoding, so 'é' (U+00E9) becomes
#                          the one Byte 233, not the two UTF-8 bytes 0xC3
#                          0xA9 -- see BYTE-SET.md. Propagates from_int's
#                          own declared error for any char outside Byte's
#                          domain (e.g. 'Ā', U+0100, or any astral
#                          character): no partial set. BelowRange is not
#                          declared: char::codepoint's own result is always
#                          nonnegative, so from_int's BelowRange branch is
#                          statically proven unreachable from here, generically
#                          (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md), not by a Byte-
#                          specific rule.
#
# ASCII/encoding interpretation, `is_unreserved`, and URI grammar are
# deliberately absent: `byte::` holds only operations intrinsic to byte/bit
# representation (core/scalarbits.tcl's header, and the milestone's own
# namespace discipline -- see BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md).
namespace byte

# The two declared errors byte::from_int's Int -> Byte conversion may
# produce (EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md item 23): application code
# meaningfully benefits from distinguishing "too small" from "too large"
# (a caller can clamp each direction differently, or report which bound was
# violated), so this chooses the two-error granularity over a single
# generic OutOfRange -- the smallest useful distinction, not the smallest
# possible one.
error BelowRange
error AboveRange

# The four scalar domains this milestone's own byte/bit-domain operations
# are typed over. These used to be compiler-side Tcl registrations
# (core/scalarbits.tcl); they are ordinary Botlish source declarations now
# (see SOURCE-DEFINED-INTEGER-DOMAINS.md) -- the compiler knows what a
# bounded integer refinement *is* (a named subtype of Int, or of another
# such subtype, whose values are exactly an inclusive interval or a finite
# set of integers); this file is what says Byte/Nibble/LowNibble/HighNibble
# *are* ones, and what their own domains are. No downstream machinery
# (result-contract verification, range/exact-set analysis, checked
# constructors, native lowering) treats these four names specially:
# everything below is the same generic feature any other source-defined
# "type Child = Parent in Domain" declaration gets.
#
#   Byte        0..255                a plain 8-bit value
#   Nibble      0..15                 a logical 4-bit value
#   LowNibble   0..15                 a nibble already positioned in the low
#                                     half of a Byte (numerically identical
#                                     to Nibble's domain, but a distinct,
#                                     nominal type: LowNibble means "already
#                                     positioned", Nibble means "logical
#                                     value" -- see item 6/74 of the report)
#   HighNibble  {0,16,32,...,240}     a nibble positioned in the high half of
#                                     a Byte -- 16 exact values, not every
#                                     integer in [0,240]
type Byte = Int in 0..255

type Nibble = Byte in 0..15
type LowNibble = Byte in 0..15

type HighNibble = Byte in {
    0, 16, 32, 48,
    64, 80, 96, 112,
    128, 144, 160, 176,
    192, 208, 224, 240
}

fn high_nibble(b) -> HighNibble:
    bit_and(b, 240)

fn low_nibble(b) -> LowNibble:
    bit_and(b, 15)

fn nibble(x) -> Nibble:
    bit_and(bit_or(bit_and(x, 15), shift_right(x, 4)), 15)

fn complement(b) -> Byte:
    bit_and(bit_xor(b, 255), 255)

fn position_low(n) -> LowNibble:
    bit_and(n, 15)

fn position_high(n) -> HighNibble:
    bit_and(shift_left(n, 4), 240)

# Int -> Byte: the ordinary explicitly-fallible conversion. VALUE is
# declared "value: int" (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md item 3): a declared
# parameter type demands *static* proof at every call site
# (STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md) -- sound here because every actual call
# site in this codebase now has a statically provable Int (or Byte, itself
# a subtype) in hand, thanks to this same milestone's own producer-side
# fixes (encode_utf8's truthful List[Byte] result, char::codepoint's own
# int result), rather than weakening this conversion back to an untyped
# escape hatch to accommodate an under-typed caller.
#
# The call-specific completion proof (hir/completions.tcl) computes each
# exact call site's own *effective* error set from its own argument facts:
# a statically provable in-range value (byte::from_int(48)) needs no
# handler and no enclosing "errors" declaration at all (its own declared
# BelowRange/AboveRange are both proven impossible for that one call); a
# statically provable out-of-range literal (byte::from_int(999)) is a
# compile-time KNOWN-ERROR, not a call a handler can make legal. A
# genuinely dynamic value keeps its full runtime error-completion
# obligation, produces the matching declared error when actually out of
# range, and never truncates or masks.
fn from_int(value: int) -> Byte errors BelowRange, AboveRange:
    if value < 0:
        fail BelowRange
    else:
        if value > 255:
            fail AboveRange
        else:
            value

# List[UnicodeChar] -> ImmutableSet[Byte]: each char's own Unicode scalar
# value (char::codepoint), converted via from_int above. Never UTF-8: a
# scalar value outside 0..255 propagates from_int's own declared error (no
# handler needed here -- it simply propagates, item 28), not truncation,
# masking, or encoding into more than one Byte -- see BYTE-SET.md. No
# partial set: the first invalid element's error ends the loop immediately.
#
# Declares only AboveRange, not BelowRange (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md
# item 23): char::codepoint's own result is a Unicode scalar value, always
# nonnegative, so from_int's BelowRange branch can never actually be
# reached from here -- the call-specific completion proof
# (hir/completions.tcl) verifies this generically from char::codepoint's
# own static facts and from_int's own body, not from any Byte-specific
# rule, every time this function's own body is checked against its
# declaration.
fn set(chars: List[UnicodeChar]) -> ImmutableSet[Byte] errors AboveRange:
    bytes = loop c in chars:
        from_int(char::codepoint(c))
    immutable_set_from_list(bytes)
